cmupmexce010gsopio5g7m26s	cmupl8l0700a2kur44tabfr5v	Measurement of Charged-Particle Production in s NN = 9.62 TeV Proton-Oxygen Collisions as a Probe of Cosmic-Ray Air Showers with the ATLAS Detector	2026	\N	\N	\N	This Letter presents a measurement of prompt charged-particle production in proton-oxygen interactions at s NN = 9.62 TeV center-of-mass energy with the ATLAS detector, corresponding to 634 μ b − 1 of integrated luminosity. A total of 246 × 10 6 selected events have at least one track with transverse momentum p T > 500 MeV and pseudorapidity | η | < 2.5 . The measured fiducial proton-oxygen cross section is σ fid . p O = 396 ± 6 ( exp . ) ± 9 ( lumi . ) mb and the extrapolated inelastic proton-air cross section is σ inel . p + air = 406 ± 6 ( exp . ) ± 9 ( lumi . ) ± 28 ( th . ) mb . Measurements of charged-particle multiplicity, p T , and η distributions are an order-of-magnitude more precise than differences between hadronic-interaction models. These results enable improved modeling of cosmic-ray air showers, which is important for astroparticle physics.	https://doi.org/10.1103/f3nk-5lt9	W7202291586	\N	https://openalex.org/W7202291586	2026-10-01 14:19:42.543	2026-10-05 05:37:49.045
cmupme1h300n2sopicysy0q32	cmupme0ns00mlsopim0ybjubv	Housing choice determinants of rural-urban migrants modeling impacts of gender and rural properties in China	2026	\N	\N	\N	Cities serve as key engines of economic growth and social vitality, with rural-urban migrants playing a crucial role in urban expansion and complex internal structural transformation. As a core domain of urban resource allocation, the accessibility and the choice of housing are influenced not only by individual user-oriented characteristics, but also act as important indicators for the growing socio-economic complexity within urban systems. Particularly in the current context of rapid urbanization, housing choices among rural-urban migrants have become a vital lens through which to understand urban spatial heterogeneity and the dynamic, often nonlinear mechanisms of social integration. In China, housing issues among rural-urban migrants are especially prominent, as they are closely intertwined with the desired goal of achieving urban citizenship (“shiminhua”). Previous studies have primarily focused on the dichotomy between renting and purchasing houses, with relatively less attention given to diverse living modes such as unit-provided housing and affordable housing. This study utilizes data from the 2017 China Migrants Dynamic Survey and applies a multinomial logistic regression m	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jum.2026.04.011	W7161260525	\N	https://openalex.org/W7161260525	2026-10-01 14:19:01.24	2026-10-05 05:36:58.501
cmupme2h500ndsopiz6arkvy7	cmupme1ou00n4sopi3lcs37np	UpTCR: a unified progressive knowledge transfer foundation model for robust T-cell receptor-antigen binding recognition	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract T-cell receptor (TCR) recognition of antigenic peptides presented by human leukocyte antigen (HLA) molecules underpins adaptive immunity and T cell-based immunotherapy. However, the scarcity of complete interaction data and TCR cross-reactivity challenge robust prediction. Here, we present UpTCR, a unified progressive knowledge-transfer foundation model that learns from incomplete data to predict TCR-antigen-HLA binding. UpTCR progressively transfers knowledge from dimeric and trimeric interactions to tetrameric complexes and uses soft contrastive learning to mitigate false negatives. It outperforms existing methods in predicting TCR binding specificity and antigen-HLA binding affinity, particularly for neoantigens, and reveals pairwise residue-level interactions across the tetramer. UpTCR also transfers effectively to breast cancer cohorts with limited data. Prospective validation against melanoma antigen variants identifies eight immunogenic peptides that elicit T cell responses and one variant associated with immune escape. These findings establish UpTCR as a generalizable and interpretable tool for studying antigen recognition and advancing TCR-based immunotherapies.	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-78075-x	W7214168946	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214168946	2026-10-01 14:19:02.537	2026-10-05 05:37:00.31
