cmupme2hh00nlsopitxdarrz3	cmupme1ou00n4sopi3lcs37np	Next-generation sequencing in the etiological diagnosis of severe childhood pneumonia	2026	\N	\N	\N	Pneumonia is the leading cause of death for children, particularly those aged 3-5 years old. The majority of the 740,000 annual global deaths of children under five years old occurred in developing countries in 2019. Severe childhood pneumonia (SCAP) causes death and disability in children, as it may trigger pleural effusion, respiratory failure, bacteremia and multiple organ failure. Traditional pathogen detection techniques including microscopy, culture and polymerase chain reaction (PCR) feature narrow detection coverage, lengthy detection cycles and low sensitivity. These defects frequently lead to undetermined infectious etiology, forcing clinicians to administer empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics. Metagenomic next-generation sequencing (mNGS) has emerged as an innovative, high-throughput, untargeted detection technology in recent years, which greatly promotes the etiological diagnosis of infectious diseases. Published meta-analyses show that the pathogen detection rate of mNGS using bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) samples collected from children can reach 85.83%, which is markedly higher than the 49.97% detection rate of conventional testing approaches. This review syst	https://doi.org/10.3389/fped.2026.1864412	W7204872787	\N	https://openalex.org/W7204872787	2026-10-01 14:19:02.55	2026-10-05 05:37:00.657
cmupme3lu00nwsopicenapgcp	cmupme2p700nnsopic5tvn90h	Dynamic regulation in solar interfacial evaporation	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract Solar interfacial evaporation (SIE) has emerged as a promising strategy for sustainable water purification by localizing solar-driven photothermal conversion at the air–liquid interface. However, most conventional SIE systems rely on static structural optimization and therefore exhibit limited adaptability under fluctuating environmental conditions. This Review presents SIE as a dynamically regulated multiphysics system governed by the coupled interactions among energy flow, mass transport, boundary-layer transport, and material responses. We summarize recent advances in dynamic regulation of light harvesting, thermal management, water transport, salt evolution, and vapor transport, and further discuss their integration through energy–mass coupling, water–ion coupling, material-mediated coupling, and hierarchical coupling. Finally, we outline future directions toward predictive multiphysics models, adaptive responsive materials, intelligent system integration, and scalable real-world deployment. By shifting the focus from optimizing individual transport processes to coordinating their interactions, this Review provides a unified framework for developing adaptive solar-driv	https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwag580	W7211883352	\N	https://openalex.org/W7211883352	2026-10-01 14:19:04.003	2026-10-05 05:37:02.504
