cmupmifou02hvsopikp3f69k6	cmupmiews02hmsopip6hoaun9	Carbon-vacancy-derived sub-nanopores as electronic regulators of Mn–N–C single-atom catalysts for alkaline oxygen reduction	2026	\N	\N	\N	Pore engineering of carbon substrates is essential for maximizing the performance of single-atom catalysts, with mesopores widely recognized as efficient mass-transport channels. By contrast, micropores, particularly sub-nanometer pores, are often regarded merely as inaccessible voids, and their role in regulating active-site chemistry remains insufficiently understood. Herein, a carbon-defect-guided sub-nanoporous Mn–N–C catalyst was developed, in which strong-alkali activation generated abundant carbon vacancies and sub-nanopores that stabilized Mn–N sites and reconstructed their local electronic environment. The resulting Mn-C Tubes exhibited a half-wave potential of 0.893 V vs. RHE and a Tafel slope of 70.1 mV dec −1 in alkaline media, together with superior durability and methanol tolerance compared with commercial 20 wt% Pt/C. Density functional theory calculations revealed that enlarging carbon vacancies progressively optimized the adsorption of oxygenated intermediates, shifted the potential-determining step, and increased the limiting potential. This work provides theoretical guidance for understanding how defect-induced sub-nanopores regulate catalytic activity.	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.carbon.2026.122118	W7213393261	\N	https://openalex.org/W7213393261	2026-10-01 14:22:26.286	2026-10-05 05:49:20.397
