cmupmsvvq00mita0ynmd2k0ux	cmupmsv0300m7ta0y7iugqicp	Multi-Feature Fusion Based Adaptive Surge Detection Method for Aero-Engine Compressors	2026	\N	\N	\N	Compressor surge poses a critical safety risk for aero-engines. However, conventional physics-driven detection methods—relying on single-domain features and fixed empirical thresholds—struggle to adapt across varying compressor configurations, wide operating ranges, and complex interference environments. This paper proposes a multi-feature fusion adaptive surge detection method that integrates time-domain amplitude, frequency-weighted power and slope features within a joint threshold criteria, enabling reliable and adaptive surge detection according to the statistical characteristics of the signal itself. A wavelet-based preprocessing strategy is established with the db4 wavelet and four-level decomposition identified as the optimal setting through systematic evaluation. A novel feature FWP is introduced herein, which applies frequency-dependent weighting to the power spectral density to suppress noise components while amplifying energy changes within surge-relevant bands, achieving 1.7 to 6.1 times greater magnitude variation near the surge point compared with total spectral power. The slope feature is further discovered to distinguish surge from transient interferences such as ra	https://doi.org/10.3390/aerospace13080734	W7203688941	\N	https://openalex.org/W7203688941	2026-10-01 14:30:33.83	2026-10-05 05:41:51.914
cmupmsvvt00mkta0y8jgfj667	cmupmsv0300m7ta0y7iugqicp	Surfactant Chain Length Driven Selectivity Switch between C 1 and C 2+ Products in CO 2 Electroreduction on Copper	2026	\N	\N	\N	Electrochemical CO 2 reduction (ECR) on copper offers a promising route to convert CO 2 into value-added chemicals, yet the product selectivity is difficult to control. Here, we develop a surfactant modification strategy to systematically tune the electrode/electrolyte interface using a series of alkyltrimethylammonium bromides (Cx-TAB, x = 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, and 18) on Cu 2 O-derived Cu electrodes. ECR performance shows a selectivity switch controlled by the surfactant chain length. The Faradaic efficiency (FE) of C 2+ products follows a volcano-type trend with an optimum at C8-TAB, increasing from 41.0% on unmodified Cu to 55.5% on C8-TAB-Cu, whereas the FE of C 1 products increases with chain length and reaches 78.6% for C18-TAB-Cu in comparison to 31.5% over Cu. In situ investigation and electrochemical analysis indicate that Cx-TAB functionalization accelerates interfacial kinetics of ECR and regulates interfacial hydration, which suppresses hydrogen evolution and shifts the key adsorbed intermediates, thus exhibiting the chain-length-dependent selectivity relationship.	https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.iecr.6c01578	W7168180637	\N	https://openalex.org/W7168180637	2026-10-01 14:30:33.833	2026-10-05 05:41:51.924
cmupmsvvw00mmta0y2bi8cq74	cmupmsv0300m7ta0y7iugqicp	Multifunctional surface engineering of copper for enhanced C 2+ production in CO 2 electroreduction	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract Electrocatalytic CO 2 reduction to high-value multi-carbon (C 2+ ) products is pivotal for sustainable carbon utilization. Copper (Cu)-based catalysts are promising for promoting electrocatalytic CO 2 reduction to yield C 2+ products. However, their performance is hindered by the competitive hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and non-optimal *CO adsorption, which severely limit both selectivity and efficiency. Here, we report a surface modification strategy employing 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTES) to functionalize Cu nanosheets. The resulting APTES/Cu-5% catalyst achieves a superior faradaic efficiency of 79.4% and high partial current density of −714.6 mA cm −2 toward C 2+ products, significantly outperforming unmodified Cu and the majority of state-of-the-art Cu catalysts. Mechanistic studies reveal that this enhanced performance arises from the dual functinality of APTES. Its –NH 2 groups facilitate CO 2 activation, while its hydrophobic siloxane backbone suppresses HER by impeding H 2 O transport. The synergistic effect enriches *CO intermediates on the Cu surface, facilitating C−C coupling and enabling high-rate C 2+ production.	https://doi.org/10.1088/2515-7655/ae8888	W7167814472	\N	https://openalex.org/W7167814472	2026-10-01 14:30:33.837	2026-10-05 05:41:51.933
