cmupmwjt90282ta0ynfhnc3nh	cmupmwixo027rta0yywct251n	Beyond the “Artificial Lakes” Paradigm: Recognizing Reservoirs as Impounded Rivers in Evaporation Estimation	2026	\N	\N	\N	ABSTRACT This review synthesizes recent studies on reservoir evaporation and shows that most studies treat reservoirs as lakes—a paradigm common in remote‐sensing‐driven regional and global assessments. Site‐specific research, however, highlights inherent differences between reservoirs and natural lakes, notably in longitudinal advection and microclimate characteristics. Attempts to adopt riverine evaporation approaches have been constrained by strong regional heterogeneity and reliance on hard‐to‐obtain parameters, limiting their transferability. Consequently, the lake‐based paradigm is inadequate for river‐type reservoirs; their hybrid river–lake behavior needs explicit representation and dedicated estimation methods. Clarifying the distinct controls on evaporation in reservoirs versus natural lakes will enhance estimation accuracy and better support water‐resources management. Priority research directions include the quantitative representation of longitudinal advection processes; the development of mass‐transfer formulations tailored to reservoir conditions; and the optimization of observation strategies to account for short fetches and complex topography.	https://doi.org/10.1002/wat2.70088	W7207640077	\N	https://openalex.org/W7207640077	2026-10-01 14:33:24.813	2026-10-01 14:33:24.813
