cmuqpr66w00gff79cypvcidrt	cmuqpr4qg00g2f79c1iznqqct	Hybridized Flat Band-Assisted Photoresponse Enhancement in Superatomic Crystal Mg4C60	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract The band structure critically determines the intrinsic photodetector performance. With a high density of states (DOS), favorable band configuration, and strong electronic correlations, flat-band physics offers promising perspectives for optoelectronic optimization. Here, we demonstrate a broadband photodetector based on Mg4C60 superatomic crystals, in which hybridization of C-2p and Mg-3s orbitals creates flat bands with a suitable semiconducting gap. Such hybridization produces a global off-Γ conduction band minimum (CBM) with a nearly flat dispersion of ∼50 meV and an indirect quasiparticle gap of ∼1.37 eV, yielding a pronounced photoresponse peak at 905 nm. Complementary optical measurements and theoretical calculations show that these flat bands promote the photodetection figures of merit in few-layer Mg4C60 devices, leading to excellent photoresponsivity of ∼7 A·W–1, detectivity of ∼8.1 × 109 Jones, noise equivalent power of ∼5.5 × 10–14 W·Hz–1/2, and external quantum efficiency of ∼950%, exceeding most carbon-based photodetectors. This work establishes a general strategy for high-performance optoelectronics via flat-band engineering in superatomic crystals.	https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.nanolett.6c03080	W7213950899	\N	https://openalex.org/W7213950899	2026-10-02 08:40:58.905	2026-10-05 05:26:10.782
cmuqpr67000ghf79cy64qh02w	cmuqpr4qg00g2f79c1iznqqct	van der Waals magnetic single-atomic chains kinetically accessible on surfaces	2026	\N	\N	\N	Isolated van der Waals (vdW) single-atomic chains represent a low-dimensional extreme with thermal stability and tunability distinct from layered and bulk materials. However, their experimental realization remains challenging because one-dimensional (1D) motifs are typically thermodynamically disfavored relative to higher-dimensional phases. Here, we perform high-throughput first-principles screening among 6,832 theoretical transition-metal chalcogenide and halide candidates, constructed from binary combinations of 28 metals and 8 non-metals. By comparing finite 1D motifs against competing two-dimensional polymorphs at the nucleation stage across experimentally relevant chemical-potential ranges, we identify 183 kinetically accessible chains. Interpretable machine-learning analysis reveals stoichiometry and metal-anion electronegativity contrast as primary drivers of 1D accessibility. Focusing on magnetic potential, we identify diverse magnetic characters and pronounced magnetoelastic couplings, exemplified by CrTe with a giant magnetostriction of 5.93%. Selected metallic ferromagnetic chains retain robust edge magnetism on vdW superconducting substrates, motivating a potential pla	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.newton.2026.100681	W7214080450	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214080450	2026-10-02 08:40:58.909	2026-10-05 05:26:10.792
cmuqpr67400gjf79c8fl7y6a4	cmuqpr4qg00g2f79c1iznqqct	Investigation of rare-earth ion-photon interaction and strong coupling in optical microcavities	2026	\N	\N	\N	Strong coupling between an emitter and a cavity is fundamental for quantum networks, enabling deterministic quantum state transfer, distributed quantum computing, and on-demand entanglement generation. Rare-earth ions (REIs) are a compelling candidate for such networks due to their long optical and spin coherence times. However, their inherently weak intra-4f optical transitions typically result in low coupling strengths, restricting most current achievements to the weak coupling regime. Our work proposes a realistic approach for achieving strong coupling between rare-earth ions and high-quality (Q) whispering-gallery-mode(WGM) microcavities. Our simulations further demonstrate that electro-optic tuning in this microdisk resonator system enables precise frequency alignment between rare-earth ions and the optical cavity mode across arbitrary target frequencies. Additionally, we provide numerical validation for a parametric amplification technique to enhance the emitter-cavity coupling strength. As an extension of this approach, the coupled system efficiently achieves the quantum entanglement of local and flying qubits. This study deepens the understanding of emitter–cavity interacti	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jlumin.2026.122101	W4415158335	\N	https://openalex.org/W4415158335	2026-10-02 08:40:58.912	2026-10-05 05:26:10.803
