cmuqpzkd002g6f79cl2ona4wa	cmuqpziw302fxf79cv642n575	Vine‐Inspired Soft Robotics: Materials, Actuations, and Emerging Applications	2026	\N	\N	\N	Inspired by the tip-growth behavior of natural vines, vine-inspired robots have emerged as a distinctive paradigm in soft robotics, enabling adaptive extension and navigation in confined, fragile, and highly unstructured environments. Unlike conventional robots that rely on whole-body locomotion, vine-inspired robots extend by generating or deploying their body from the tip, making performance strongly dependent on material properties, interfaces, and processing strategies. This review provides a comprehensive overview of recent advances from a materials perspective. We first examine material strategies for fluid-driven eversion and phase-transition- or deposition-enabled growth, linking material chemistry, geometry, interfaces, and phase behavior to growth performance. We then discuss how material-structure design supports steering, retraction, multifunctional tip design, variable stiffness, climbing and attachment, and branching, while evaluating their performance trade-offs and technological maturity. Representative applications in medical diagnosis and intervention, navigation and exploration, transport and operational tasks, and deployable and reconfigurable structures are hig	https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.78022	W7214966559	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214966559	2026-10-02 08:47:30.517	2026-10-03 11:07:10.449
cmuqpzkda02g8f79cr4hlawye	cmuqpziw302fxf79cv642n575	Single-Molecule Electron Transport on C60–PtSe2 Organic–2D Transition Metal Dichalcogenide Heterointerface	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract Charge transport across organic–inorganic heterointerfaces plays a pivotal role in molecular electronics, yet remains insufficiently understood at the molecular scale. Here, we investigate single-molecule Au–C60–PtSe2 junctions using the STM break-junction technique under ambient conditions, complemented by DFT-NEGF calculations. Compared with symmetric Au–C60–Au junctions, the Au–C60–PtSe2 junction exhibits a lower conductance by approximately two orders of magnitude (∼120×) and a current rectification ratio of 5.2. Current–voltage measurements reveal asymmetric charge transport, which is well described by a single-level transport model. The extracted parameters indicate extremely weak electronic coupling at the C60–PtSe2 interface relative to the Au–C60 contact. Theoretical calculations identify the C60–PtSe2 contact as the dominant transport bottleneck despite the semimetallic character of the PtSe2 electrode. These results quantitatively resolve charge transport across an individual organic–transition metal dichalcogenide (TMD) contact and highlight the key role of interfacial coupling in governing molecular transport at conducting layered electrodes.	https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jpcc.6c04732	W7212167737	\N	https://openalex.org/W7212167737	2026-10-02 08:47:30.527	2026-10-03 11:07:10.468
