cmuqq3ct203duf79c8uure8x0	cmuqq3bc403dhf79cxh4a6fsq	Dipole–dipole interaction facilitates anion-rich solvation structures in polymer electrolytes for solid-state lithium metal batteries	2026	\N	\N	\N	Polymer electrolytes are vital for safe and high-energy–density lithium metal batteries but face challenges from interfacial instability and Li dendrites. Here, we report a molecular design exploiting dipole–dipole interactions to tailor Li + solvation structures and interfacial chemistry. Incorporating pentafluorophenyl acrylate into the polymer backbone enhances dipole interactions with 1,2-dimethoxyethane solvent, modulating the solvation structure and promoting anion entry into the primary coordination sphere. The anion-rich environment facilitates a durable, LiF-dominated solid electrolyte interphase, effectively suppressing dendrites and improving interfacial stability. The resulting electrolyte enables stable lithium plating/stripping over 2000 hours in symmetric cells and extends Li||LiNi 0.9 Co 0.05 Mn 0.05 O 2 (NCM90) full cell cycling to 1000 cycles at 1.0 C. An anode-free Cu||NCM90 pouch cell delivers a high-energy–density of 579 Wh kg −1 . This study underscores the critical role of dipole-mediated solvation structuring in polymer electrolytes and offers a generalizable approach toward high-performance, energy-dense solid-state batteries.	https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aej0175	W7213532429	\N	https://openalex.org/W7213532429	2026-10-02 08:50:27.35	2026-10-05 05:34:16.527
cmuqq3ct603dwf79cfzzzaxzw	cmuqq3bc403dhf79cxh4a6fsq	Nonadiabatic Vibronic Dynamics at a Conical Intersection with Morse Potentials	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract Conical intersections (CIs) are primary pathways for ultrafast nonradiative relaxation in photoexcited molecules, yet their spectroscopic signatures often reflect a complex interplay of electronic motion, vibrational dynamics, and environmental dephasing. This challenge becomes particularly significant when the nuclear coordinate driving the relaxation is strongly anharmonic, since harmonic approximations commonly employed in CI models cannot capture the asymmetric level structure and wave-packet evolution associated with realistic bondstretching motion. Here, we develop a dissipative two-state, two-mode vibronic model that combines an anharmonic Morse tuning coordinate with a harmonic coupling coordinate and a three-level extension for simulating two-dimensional electronic–vibrational (2DEV) spectroscopy. Reduced density-matrix dynamics are propagated using the hierarchy equations of motion, while absorptive 2DEV spectra are calculated within a phase-matching formalism. To isolate the effect of Morse anharmonicity, we further introduce a parameter-matched fully harmonic reference model with the same local frequencies, potential-surface displacements, electronic coupling, 	https://doi.org/10.1088/1674-1056/aea538	W7212167575	\N	https://openalex.org/W7212167575	2026-10-02 08:50:27.354	2026-10-05 05:34:17.089
cmuqq3cta03dyf79c8cw8nebx	cmuqq3bc403dhf79cxh4a6fsq	Discovering solid-state superionic conductors with low Li-ion concentration assisted by artificial intelligence	2026	\N	\N	\N	Superionic conductors (SICs) are essential for solid-state Li-ion batteries, yet their practical application is hindered by high Li-ion concentrations that increase inactive Li and battery cost. Here, we develop an artificial intelligence-driven discovery framework integrating a large language model and graph neural network to accelerate SIC design. The large language model, GPT-4o, extracts key transport parameters from 465 peer-reviewed studies, identifying oxyhalides as promising candidates. A materials space of 69,920 oxyhalides spanning 230 space groups is constructed, and ionic conductivity is predicted using a graph neural network. Compounds with gravimetric and volumetric Li-ion concentrations below 3.05 mol kg −1 and 11.49 mol L −1 , respectively, as well as ionic conductivities above 10 mS cm −1 are further validated via ab initio molecular dynamics simulations. Notably, LiMoOBr 4 exhibits a low Li-ion concentration (2.28 mol kg −1 ) and high ionic conductivity (13.53 mS cm −1 ). This work accelerates the design of advanced SICs for battery utilization.	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpblue.2026.100123	W7212050957	\N	https://openalex.org/W7212050957	2026-10-02 08:50:27.358	2026-10-05 05:34:17.373
