cmusa86cs029y6ojw5ys0yk1n	cmusa85fg029l6ojw2ddwebud	Comprehensive Analysis of Torsional Vibration Response in Marine Propulsion Shafting Systems	2026	\N	\N	\N	The marine propulsion shafting system serves as the core component of ship power transmission, wherein torsional vibrations can easily lead to shaft cracking and failure. Thus, avoiding shafting resonance is vital for ship safety. Previous research primarily focuses on a single vibration mechanism of diesel engine propulsion shafting systems, lacking a comprehensive analysis of modal characteristics, frequency, and transient responses. This paper systematically investigates the torsional vibration characteristics of shafting systems, constructs a mathematical model for torsional vibrations, deduces a method for solving natural frequencies, and establishes a frequency-domain transfer function matrix using the Laplace Transform to theoretically derive the transient response of damped forced vibrations. Taking the propulsion shafting system of a low-speed diesel engine in a 10,000-ton oil tanker as an example, a multi-condition analysis based on a simplified shafting model is conducted. This includes modal solution analysis, 0–2000 Hz frequency sweep tests, and comparative experiments on transient responses under different excitation frequencies with a 1000 Nm torque. The study reveal	https://doi.org/10.4271/2026-99-1274	W7207645957	\N	https://openalex.org/W7207645957	2026-10-03 11:01:50.765	2026-10-05 05:42:40.191
cmusa86d002a06ojwktd7xcvp	cmusa85fg029l6ojw2ddwebud	Learning intention formation mechanisms among university students in virtual heritage experiences of Sichuan shadow puppetry	2026	\N	\N	\N	Shadow puppetry, an intangible cultural heritage (ICH), urgently requires digital technology to enrich learning experiences. Although virtual reality (VR) offers immersive interactions for ICH education, user experience mechanisms remain under-researched. Based on a Sichuan shadow puppetry VR platform, this study integrated the Stimulus–Organism–Response (S-O-R) framework, Information Systems Success Model (ISSM), and Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) to build a VR learning intention model for university students. Analyzing 522 empirical datasets via structural equation modeling (SEM) and artificial neural network (ANN), we found that content quality (CQ) and interaction quality (INQ) significantly enhanced perceived ease of use (PEOU) and perceived usefulness (PU), whereas service quality (SEQ) showed negligible effects. PEOU and PU drove learning intention (LI) through learning attitude (LA), with self-efficacy (SE) strengthening the attitude–intention transition. ANN further revealed non-linear predictive complexities. These insights inform digital ICH pedagogy and VCH system design.	https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-026-02957-y	W7208739765	\N	https://openalex.org/W7208739765	2026-10-03 11:01:50.773	2026-10-05 05:42:40.206
