cmusa8iop02en6ojws1vxpr7v	cmusa8hlc02ec6ojwwohz7wtx	A-site ionic size modulates bulk exchange interaction and surface reconstruction for enhanced oxygen evolution	2026	\N	\N	\N	Regulating catalytic activity through non-active lattice sites represents a compelling yet underexplored strategy in transition metal oxides. In ABO 3 perovskites, this opportunity has been largely overlooked because conventional approaches predominantly focus on directly tuning the catalytically active B-site cations, whereas the role of A-site ions in governing bulk electronic interactions and surface reconstruction remains poorly understood. Here, by systematically tuning the A-site cation from larger to smaller ions (A = La, Sr, Ca, Sm), we demonstrate that the A-site ionic size in ACoO 3 perovskites serves as a key tuning parameter to remotely modulate catalytic behavior across multiple length scales. As the A-site ionic radius decreases, lattice distortion progressively intensifies, leading to lattice contraction and increased Co oxidation state. This structural evolution modifies bulk electronic interactions, including charge transport and exchange coupling, which in turn regulate surface reconstruction behavior under OER conditions. Notably, although all samples undergo reconstruction toward Co-based oxyhydroxide species, the reconstructed surface retains key electronic cha	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2026.181683	W7210264460	\N	https://openalex.org/W7210264460	2026-10-03 11:02:06.746	2026-10-05 05:42:56.852
