cmusafqhj03zi6ojwuswxvpd4	cmusafpgp03z96ojw0brpc0rl	Social influences on the adoption of electric vehicles: A meta-analysis	2026	\N	\N	\N	This study conducts a meta‑analysis of 152 primary estimates to examine how social influences affect electric vehicle (EV) adoption. We distinguish between informational influences (social interaction and neighborhood effect) and normative influences (social norm and social symbolism). The results confirm a significant overall effect of social influences on EV adoption, with normative influences outweighing informational ones. Over time, the dominant normative driver shifts from social symbolism to social norm. Cross‑sectionally, social norm is more influential in collectivistic cultures and for conservative innovations, whereas informational social interaction is more influential in individualistic cultures and for radical innovations. Based on these findings, we recommend celebrity endorsements to amplify social symbolism in early market stages, and green labelling to reinforce social norm in mature markets. Community-based campaigns that foster social interaction are suggested for individualistic regions and radical innovations, while broad publicity emphasizing social norm is suggested for collectivistic regions and conservative innovations.	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.trd.2026.105647	W7214111205	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214111205	2026-10-03 11:07:43.447	2026-10-03 11:07:43.447
