cmusajblt04nl6ojw4d6w3j6q	cmusajaxc04nc6ojw0q2kmi0s	AI-accelerated discovery of altermagnetic metal-organic frameworks via interpretable machine learning	2026	\N	\N	\N	Altermagnetism has emerged as a third class of collinear magnetism, yet its exploration remains largely confined to inorganic materials. Here, we establish an AI-accelerated pipeline for discovering altermagnetic metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). From symmetry-guided DFT screening of 350 candidates, we identified six altermagnetic MOFs. A two-stage machine learning strategy was then employed: First, an XGBoost classifier pre-screened 65,578 candidates, narrowing the pool to 145 high-potential linkers, from which DFT confirmed six new altermagnets. Building on this enriched dataset, we trained a KNN classifier (F1 = 0.8793) and a CatBoost regressor ( R ² = 0.7732) for predicting altermagnetism and spin-splitting magnitudes, respectively. SHAP analysis translated predictions into design principles: conjugated backbones favor altermagnetism, while moderate push-pull groups enhance spin splitting. Guided by these principles, we designed three additional MOF candidates, all confirmed by DFT. In total, this workflow identified 15 altermagnetic MOFs, establishing a generalizable framework for accelerating quantum materials discovery.	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41524-026-02323-3	W7212783671	\N	https://openalex.org/W7212783671	2026-10-03 11:10:30.785	2026-10-03 11:10:30.785
cmusajbm404nn6ojw2a2finb9	cmusajaxc04nc6ojw0q2kmi0s	Distinguishing Majorana zero modes from trivial defect states in an iron-based superconductor	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract Majorana zero modes, which obey non-Abelian exchange statistics, are promising candidates for topological quantum computation due to their robustness against environmental perturbations. The iron-based superconductor Fe(Te,Se) has been identified as an intrinsic topological superconductor, possibly hosting Majorana zero modes. In this paper, we report the observation of near-zero-energy localized states at multiple structural defects on the Fe(Te,Se) surface, which could be misidentified as Majorana zero modes without additional verification. By using spin-polarized scanning tunneling spectroscopy, we demonstrate that the near-zero-energy localized states on step edges and line defects originate from topologically trivial Yu-Shiba-Rusinov states. In addition, zero-energy bound states are also observed for regions without surface defects. A combined spatial and magnetic field dependent analysis of the spin-resolved tunneling spectra in these regions reveals that this type of zero-energy states cannot be attributed to the presence of Majorana bound states. These findings emphasize the importance of spin-dependent studies of low-energy states for pursuing Majorana zero modes.	https://doi.org/10.1038/s42005-026-02832-z	W7211910569	\N	https://openalex.org/W7211910569	2026-10-03 11:10:30.796	2026-10-03 11:10:30.796
