cmusak5i104y56ojwr2tgej0g	cmusak42h04xw6ojwm0uq0yor	Angle-based leaderless formation maneuvering with global convergence	2026	\N	\N	\N	Much of the existing research on formation maneuvering control is based on a leader-follower framework, in which one or more leaders track a desired trajectory or velocity, and the rest adapt to it. However, if a leader fails, the desired maneuvering is typically compromised. Alternatively, some studies on formation maneuvering control introduce designed mismatches within a leaderless framework, but these mismatches must be determined offline, and most such methods only achieve local convergence to the desired maneuver. To address these issues, this paper investigates the problem of angle-rigid formation maneuvering control with simultaneous scaling, rotation, and translation in leaderless multi-agent systems. Without requiring offline-designed mismatches for a predetermined maneuver, a formation maneuvering control law is proposed, generating a virtual velocity signal derived from angle constraints within the desired triangles. Combined with a distributed estimation algorithm, the proposed method ensures that the closed-loop multi-agent system globally achieves the desired angle rigid formation maneuvering in a fully distributed manner. Furthermore, to enable implementation in age	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.automatica.2026.113300	W7215029776	\N	https://openalex.org/W7215029776	2026-10-03 11:11:09.529	2026-10-03 11:11:09.529
cmusak5ip04y76ojwn1c03uca	cmusak42h04xw6ojwm0uq0yor	Complexity and delay shape oscillatory transitions in complex networks	2026	\N	\N	\N	Abstract Oscillation, as a prevalent emergent phenomenon across physical, biological, and ecological systems, is essential for the development and maintenance of many physiological and physical processes. However, the mechanisms through which propagation delays and structural parameters jointly determine the excitation or suppression of oscillatory dynamics remain poorly understood. Here, we show that variations in network complexity, delays, and interaction types provide a parametric route to tuning oscillatory transitions in complex systems. We further demonstrate that delays function as a critical, tunable parameter that complements network complexity in shaping these dynamics. Interestingly, our results reveal that cooperative interactions most effectively promote the emergence of oscillations, outperforming mixed, competitive, and random interaction schemes. Finally, we validate our theoretical predictions through hardware-in-the-loop emulations and demonstrate how network complexity and propagation delays shape oscillatory transitions in an empirically reconstructed complex-network topology.	https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwag608	W7214099546	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214099546	2026-10-03 11:11:09.553	2026-10-03 11:11:09.553
