cmuusx4hx00if8xwwck3j3nhj	cmuplb6jx017qkur4yhi7zuvp	Accelerating Regular Expression Matching over Compressed Data via Decoupled Speculative Execution on FPGA	2026	\N	\N	\N	The common practice of compressing network traffic to enhance transmission efficiency poses a significant challenge to achieving high-speed regular expression matching. Since matching compression data relies on prior decompressed data and earlier matching outcomes, the conventional approach usually scans fully decompressed data, which suffers from bottlenecks caused by data inflation. Existing approaches accelerate compressed data matching by eliminating duplicate scanning, yet this speedup incurs unavoidable overhead on general-purpose CPU architectures. This paper introduces STRIDE, an FPGA-based accelerator that resolves the aforementioned constraints via decoupled speculative execution. Specifically, by decoupling the matching of compressed encodings from both the matching of uncompressed literals and the resolution of prior decompressed data, STRIDE leverages speculative results to enable uninterrupted scanning that does not depend on earlier outputs. Then, an asynchronous verification process validates these speculations and performs necessary corrections to ensure correctness. We implement STRIDE on a Xilinx Kintex-7 XC7K325T FPGA platform. Evaluations on real-world compress	https://doi.org/10.1145/3849707	W7218523340	\N	https://openalex.org/W7218523340	2026-10-05 05:20:40.197	2026-10-05 05:48:59.901
cmuusx4l500ij8xwwpe20ccba	cmuplb6jx017qkur4yhi7zuvp	Organic Semiconductor Single Crystals: From Continuous Fabrication to Optoelectronic Applications	2026	\N	\N	\N	Organic semiconductors serve as the cornerstone for next-generation flexible, cost-effective, and large-area optoelectronics, offering profound research significance and immense industrial potential. Compared to their polycrystalline and amorphous counterparts, organic single crystals possess distinctive advantages, including long-range molecular order, minimal defect density, superior intrinsic carrier mobility, and robust environmental stability. Consequently, they provide an ideal benchmark for probing fundamental charge transport physics and developing high-performance devices. In this review, we briefly trace the evolution of organic semiconductor single crystals (OSSCs) before discussing recent breakthroughs in the field. We place particular emphasis on the crystallization kinetics, continuous fabrication techniques, carrier transport mechanisms, electronic and optoelectronic applications of OSSCs. Finally, we offer insights into future trajectories, focusing on crystallization theories, charge transport mechanisms, and device technologies compatible with scalable processing.	https://doi.org/10.3390/ma19194207	W7216458178	\N	https://openalex.org/W7216458178	2026-10-05 05:20:40.313	2026-10-05 05:48:59.937
cmuusx4qq00il8xwwljhbjp96	cmuplb6jx017qkur4yhi7zuvp	Machine Learning Assisted Approach for Characteristic Mode Tracking	2026	\N	\N	\N	ABSTRACT Characteristic mode (CM) tracking is important and challenging for CM computations, especially for complicated structures. In this paper, a machine learning (ML)‐based method is proposed to track the CM effectively and efficiently, thus removing the mode swapping at the crossing frequencies. Due to the typical time‐series (TS) characteristics of the mode data at different frequencies, a dynamic classification method based on a long short‐term memory (LSTM) neural network is exploited to classify the modes over a frequency band for mode tracking. The untracked CMs are firstly selected as the training dataset, which is updated periodically during tracking. By updating the network with the tracked mode data, the proposed method solves the mode degradation problem that often occurs in conventional mode tracking methods. To validate the method, the modes of a simple metallic plate and a complicated fractal antenna are tracked. Compared with the tracking method in Altair FEKO, the proposed LSTM‐based method provides accurate tracking results with less mesh elements, using less computational time as well.	https://doi.org/10.1002/mop.70811	W7219134460	\N	https://openalex.org/W7219134460	2026-10-05 05:20:40.514	2026-10-05 05:48:59.971
