cmuut9gjh02so8xwwrq4kxrcc	cmuqprji900l0f79chy0tjmjt	Small Community Businesses in Slums: A Perspective on Kenyan Slum Networks	2026	\N	\N	\N	ABSTRACT Despite extensive research on networks, the relational dimensions and structures cocreated by small community businesses and cooperative societies through everyday entrepreneurship in slum neighborhoods remain undertheorized. Drawing upon 1166 small community businesses, we conceptualize the relational dimensions and structures underlying their slum networks in Kenya. Network analysis suggests that these small community businesses, characterized by their distinct focus on the everyday entrepreneurship involving slum essentials—firewood, food, labor, fish, water, and farm produce—coalesce into a complex geometric axis of slum networks. Their geographical reach extends across Kenya's slum neighborhoods, but the cooperative societies they are affiliated with are confined by their immediate surroundings. Thus, extending network theories of closeness, centrality, and betweenness to incorporate the relational dimensions and structures inherent in Kenya's slums, where small community businesses and cooperative societies are the heartbeat. With over a billion people in slums, this understanding establishes a baseline for social, policy, and academic applications.	https://doi.org/10.1002/jsc.70126	W7213776848	\N	https://openalex.org/W7213776848	2026-10-05 05:30:15.678	2026-10-05 05:30:15.678
cmuut9gm202sq8xwwb9ujgbrl	cmuqprji900l0f79chy0tjmjt	To open-source or not to open-source: A theoretical model of the electric vehicle industry	2026	\N	\N	\N	We propose an open-source model with leaders and followers to theoretically study the electric vehicle (EV) industry. Our model shows that the immaturity of technology and technological competition are the two main reasons EV giants, such as Tesla and Toyota, chose open-source. Due to technological competition, these giants’ behaviors are more strategic than traditional models show, and the relative difference between different technology paths is more crucial than the absolute technology level. Open source has driven many new EV makers into the market, resulting in more technological progress. We also show that open source changes the market structure of the automotive industry such that many small firms can stay in the market as the EV industry matures. Our study extends the open-source model to a more complicated market structure that allows different technological paths and giants to coexist and compete throughout history. Thus, our model provides more strategic dynamics than previous open-source models, which can be applied to other traditional manufacturing industries that have encountered significant changes in the digital revolution.	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.iref.2026.105881	W7214920177	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214920177	2026-10-05 05:30:15.771	2026-10-05 05:30:15.771
