cmuutb94k03fw8xwwutwilz2d	cmuutb7xk03fl8xww9asj2023	Design and fabrication of a bio-inspired frog robot based on electromagnetic catapult	2025	\N	\N	\N	This paper presents an electromagnetic catapult-driven bio-inspired frog robot. Its core innovation is a novel compact limiting joint, drawing from double-parallelogram and umbrella-rib structures, enhancing control stability and force output. The drive system integrates electromagnetic coil parameter optimization (turns, voltage) for maximum jump distance using power transfer theory. Crucially, it exploits the hyperelasticity of polymer materials (e.g., rubber bands), where tensile strain increases their elastic modulus. A self-locking mechanism ensures targeted pre-stretching matches this high-stiffness phase, maximizing energy transfer efficiency. An FDM-3D printed prototype (330g mass) achieved a 6.5 m/s launch velocity and 2.9m jump distance. This robot demonstrates superior locomotion performance, control robustness, and terrain adaptability, validating its practical utility.	https://doi.org/10.1049/icp.2025.3272	W4416859631	\N	https://openalex.org/W4416859631	2026-10-05 05:31:39.38	2026-10-05 05:31:39.38
cmuutb94y03fy8xwwxzpngnbi	cmuutb7xk03fl8xww9asj2023	The sense of community for the Chinese nation promoting helping behavior among residents during COVID-19 pandemic	2025	\N	\N	\N	Helping behavior has emerged in various forms during the COVID-19 pandemic, and social identity plays a key role in shaping such behavior. The sense of community for the Chinese nation (SCCN) exemplifies social identity in the Chinese context, reflecting the unified identification with the "Chinese nation" among diverse ethnic groups. Grounded in Social Identity Theory, the current study used a large-scale questionnaire (N = 6768) to examine how SCCN fosters helping behavior. Results indicated that SCCN positively predicted helping behavior; social responsibility and perceived protection efficacy each mediated this relationship; and social responsibility and perceived protection efficacy jointly exerted a partial chain mediating effect. These findings uncover the psychological mechanisms through which SCCN promotes helping behavior in China during the pandemic and provide a referential framework for crisis response in multi-ethnic societies with shared identities. They also suggest possible pathways for nations and international organizations to enhance cooperation in addressing global challenges by strengthening social identities.	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.actpsy.2025.105543	W4414125441	\N	https://openalex.org/W4414125441	2026-10-05 05:31:39.394	2026-10-05 05:31:39.394
