cmuuttgkf06bf8xwwojcv8xjy	cmuuttfgb06b48xwwu7aog3ut	Scientific World Models: Toward Closed-Loop Scientific Intelligence	2026	\N	\N	\N	Artificial intelligence is moving from task-specific scientific tools toward foundation models, generative design, research agents, and autonomous laboratories. Despite this progress, most systems remain organized around isolated predictions or local workflows. They rarely maintain an evolving account of scientific objects, predict the consequences of interventions, or learn systematically from computational and experimental evidence. This article introduces scientific world models as a core paradigm for closed-loop scientific intelligence. A scientific world model reconstructs the physical state of a scientific object and its corresponding epistemic state—what is known, uncertain, or contested—from partial, multimodal, and instrument-mediated observations. It models action-conditioned state transitions, explores plausible futures under scientific constraints, and supports the selection of measurements, simulations, experiments, and process interventions according to scientific value, information gain, cost, feasibility, and risk. The new evidence then updates the state, hypotheses, and subsequent actions. On this basis, we propose a three-layer architecture of scientific perceptio	https://doi.org/10.26434/chemrxiv.15009602/v1	W7214748783	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214748783	2026-10-05 05:45:48.831	2026-10-05 05:45:48.831
