cmuutwt3x07gc8xww1cpt01r8	cmuutws6q07g38xwwjes8mbjp	Low‐Power Direct Photopatterning of Colloidal Quantum Dots	2026	\N	\N	\N	ABSTRACT Quantum dot light‐emitting diodes (QLEDs) require high‐resolution photopatterning of quantum dots (QDs) while preserving their optoelectronic properties, yet this remains a major challenge. Here, we systematically investigate the influence of bridge‐group conjugation on photolithographic performance by modulating the π ‐conjugation of azide crosslinkers. A highly π ‐conjugated tetraphenylethylene (TPE)‐based crosslinker exhibits a molar extinction coefficient (ɛ) exceeding 60,000 L mol − 1 cm − 1 , enabling low‐dose, non‐destructive photolithography. High‐fidelity red, green, and blue QD patterns with a resolution of 12,700 PPI are achieved under an ultralow UV exposure dose of only 3 mJ cm − 2 in air. The excellent optical retention originates from the reduced UV exposure requirement together with favorable energy‐level alignment between the crosslinker and QDs. Full‐patterned R/G/B QLEDs exhibit peak external quantum efficiencies of 20.79%, 15.27%, and 3.99%, respectively, with device performance approaching or even surpassing that of pristine devices. This highly π ‐conjugated crosslinker design provides a general strategy for low‐power, non‐destructive QD photolithogra	https://doi.org/10.1002/lpor.71955	W7214207741	\N	https://openalex.org/W7214207741	2026-10-05 05:48:25.053	2026-10-05 05:48:25.053
